#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Part 1: Li2S (20Q) {#part-1-li2s-20q}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem {#step-1-map-classical-"
"inputs-to-a-quantum-problem}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Define $Li_2S$ geometries in dictionary format for different Li-S bond "
"distances to calculate the PES curve. These geometries are optimized using "
"B3LYP/631g calculations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The HI-VQE calculations use the options defined in the following cell. With "
"the STO-3G basis, $Li_2S$ has 19 spatial orbitals and 22 electrons. To run "
"the (10o,12e) case with HI-VQE, define 10 active orbitals and freeze the "
"five lowest orbitals, which leaves 12 active electrons. At each iteration, "
"100 shots are used to sample the electron configurations generated by the "
"ExcitationPreserving quantum circuit (`epa`) with `circular` entanglement "
"and two repetitions (`reps`). The maximum number of iterations is set to 30,"
" which allows enough iterations for the energy to converge.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Steps 2 and 3: Optimize problem for quantum hardware execution and execute "
"using the HI-VQE Chemistry function {#steps-2-and-3-optimize-problem-for-"
"quantum-hardware-execution-and-execute-using-the-hi-vqe-chemistry-function}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Set up a `for` loop that submits an HI-VQE job for each geometry, with the options defined previously. In this tutorial, you submit six geometries and retrieve the results after all jobs complete. In the main function run, define `max_states` to control the maximum size of the subspace matrix, and `max_expansion_states` to control how many states classical CI expansion methods can generate per iteration. The function job IDs are stored in a dictionary under each geometry label, so that you can track and process the output.\n"
"These geometries need 20 qubits and run with `max_states=40000`, so the calculator gives `M`. They share their active space and `max_states`, so the size is calculated once.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Check](/guides/functions-get-started#check-job-status) whether the jobs are"
" still running or have completed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"After all jobs complete, [retrieve](/guides/functions-get-started#retrieve-"
"results) the calculation results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The results file for each of the calculations can be downloaded into the "
"current directory, under its own name, with `catalog.file_download()`. See "
"[Manage files across runs](/guides/manage-files-across-managed-workflows) "
"for how files are moved to and from a function. These files contain "
"`\"states\"`, `\"eigenvector\"`, `\"energy_history\"`, `\"final_energy\"`, "
"and `\"final_energy_variance\"`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The results can be read from the h5 file as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The entire QPU runtime used in the job can be tracked by logging in to [IBM "
"Quantum Platform](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/) and viewing submitted jobs"
" with the `qunova-chemistry-hivqe` tag. To find the IBM Quantum Compute jobs"
" of one function job from code, see [Access the associated Quantum Compute "
"jobs](/guides/functions-get-started#access-the-associated-quantum-compute-"
"jobs).\n"
msgstr ""

